## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about live-code updates in a running kernel, including synchronization, cache coherence, and control of instruction fetch paths under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Live patching of executable memory without stopping workers
- Per-page state machines and atomic publish
- I-cache/TLB synchronization and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal C-like sketch (pseudo-code)
enum { STABLE, STAGED, ACTIVE } state_t;
typedef struct { void *addr; void *code; state_t s; volatile int refcnt; uint64_t ver; } page_t;

int publish_patch(page_t *p, void *new_code) {
  p->s = STAGED;
  p->code = new_code;
  __sync_synchronize();
  if (atomic_compare_exchange(&p->s, STAGED, ACTIVE)) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&p->ver, 1);
    icache_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    tlb_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    return 0;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you guarantee rollback if a patch proves faulty?
- How to test latency under burst patch arrivals and mixed workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a simple physical allocator, reason about fragmentation, testability, and correctness in a beginner-friendly way; uses fundamental data structures; demonstrates thought about coalescing.

## Key Concepts
- Free-list data structure for fixed resources
- Coalescing contiguous free blocks
- Fragmentation metrics and simple tests
- Straightforward, single-threaded correctness assumptions

## Code Example
```c
#define NR_FRAMES 256
typedef struct Frame {
  int next;
  int free;
} Frame;
Frame frames[NR_FRAMES];
int free_head;

void init_allocator() {
  for (int i = 0; i < NR_FRAMES; ++i) {
    frames[i].next = i+1;
    frames[i].free = 1;
  }
  frames[NR_FRAMES-1].next = -1;
  free_head = 0;
}

int alloc_one() {
  if (free_head == -1) return -1;
  int f = free_head;
  free_head = frames[f].next;
  frames[f].free = 0;
  return f;
}

void free_block(int start, int count) {
  int end = start + count - 1;
  frames[end].next = free_head;
  free_head = start;
  for (int i = start; i <= end; ++i) frames[i].free = 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multi-threaded allocators and introduce synchronization?
- What fragmentation metrics would you track, and how would you prove improvement with coalescing?

## Why This Is Asked
