## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical I/O throttling in the kernel, including how to enforce per-process quotas across devices, handle bursts, and integrate with the block layer without starving others.

## Key Concepts

- Token bucket per process
- Refill cadence and burst handling
- Cross-device fairness (cgroups/weights)
- Enqueue/wake paths in kernel I/O
- Atomic synchronization across CPUs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal token bucket sketch (pseudo-C style in JS-like syntax for readability)
typedef struct { atomic_uint tokens; uint32_t bucket; uint32_t rate; spinlock_t lock; wait_queue_t wq; } tb_t;
bool io_submit(tb_t *tb, size_t size) {
  if (atomic_read(&tb->tokens) >= size) {
    atomic_sub(&tb->tokens, size);
    submit_to_device(size);
    return true;
  }
  enqueue_io(size);
  return false;
}
void refill(tb_t *tb) {
  uint32_t t = atomic_read(&tb->tokens) + tb->rate;
  if (t > tb->bucket) t = tb->bucket;
  atomic_write(&tb->tokens, t);
  wake_up(&tb->wq);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test latency vs throughput for mixed burst patterns?
- How to ensure fairness when devices have different speeds and tenants with varying quotas?

Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of kernel I/O paths, concurrency, and memory barriers.

Key Concepts

- Lock-free ring buffers and CAS
- Per-slot versioning to prevent double-consume
- Memory order fences between ISR, producer, and consumer
- Wakeup semantics and fairness
- Testing under bursty I/O and backpressure

Code Example

```c
// Minimal producer (ISR) enqueue
int enq(struct ring *r, struct evt *e){
  int idx = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed) & (RING_MASK);
  if (atomic_load_explicit(&r->slots[idx].claimed, memory_order_acquire))
    return -1;
  r->slots[idx].evt = *e;
  atomic_store_explicit(&r->slots[idx].claimed, 1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->head, 1, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}
```

